<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1113" typeof="mw:Transclusion" id="mwCkY" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Reflist","href":"./Template:Reflist"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"30em"}},"i":0}}]}'><style data-mw-deduplicate="TemplateStyles:r1327269900" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" about="#mwt1114" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Reflist/styles.css"}}'>.mw-parser-output .reflist-columns-2{column-width:30em}.mw-parser-output .reflist-columns-3{column-width:25em}body.skin-vector-2022 .mw-parser-output .reflist-columns-2{column-width:27em}body.skin-vector-2022 .mw-parser-output .reflist-columns-3{column-width:22.5em}.mw-parser-output .references[data-mw-group=upper-alpha]{list-style-type:upper-alpha}.mw-parser-output .references[data-mw-group=upper-roman]{list-style-type:upper-roman}.mw-parser-output .references[data-mw-group=lower-alpha]{list-style-type:lower-alpha}.mw-parser-output .references[data-mw-group=lower-greek]{list-style-type:lower-greek}.mw-parser-output .references[data-mw-group=lower-roman]{list-style-type:lower-roman}.mw-parser-output div.reflist-liststyle-upper-alpha .references{list-style-type:upper-alpha}.mw-parser-output div.reflist-liststyle-upper-roman .references{list-style-type:upper-roman}.mw-parser-output div.reflist-liststyle-lower-alpha .references{list-style-type:lower-alpha}.mw-parser-output div.reflist-liststyle-lower-greek .references{list-style-type:lower-greek}.mw-parser-output div.reflist-liststyle-lower-roman .references{list-style-type:lower-roman}</style></span><div class="mw-references-wrap mw-references-columns" style="column-width: calc( 0.9 * 30em );" about="#mwt1113" id="mwCkc"><ol class="mw-references references" typeof="mw:Extension/references" about="#mwt1115" id="mwCkg" data-mw='{"name":"references","attrs":{"group":"","responsive":"0"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'><li about="#cite_note-Abbatep3-1" id="cite_note-Abbatep3-1" data-mw-footnote-number="1"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwCkk"><a href="#cite_ref-Abbatep3_1-0" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwCko"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwCks">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-Abbatep3-1" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="#CITEREFAbbate1999" about="#mwt10" typeof="mw:Transclusion" class="mw-selflink-fragment" id="mwCkw" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"harvnb","href":"./Template:Harvnb"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Abbate"},"2":{"wt":"1999"},"p":{"wt":"[https://books.google.com/books?id=9BfZxFZpElwC&amp;pg=PA3 3] \"The manager of the ARPANET project, Lawrence Roberts, assembled a large team of computer scientists ... and he drew on the ideas of network experimenters in the United States and the United Kingdom. Cerf and Kahn also enlisted the help of computer scientists from England, France and the United States\""}},"i":0}}]}'>Abbate 1999</a><span about="#mwt10" id="mwCk0">, p.</span><span typeof="mw:Entity" about="#mwt10" id="mwCk4"> </span><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://books.google.com/books?id=9BfZxFZpElwC&amp;pg=PA3" about="#mwt10" class="external text" id="mwCk8">3</a><span about="#mwt10" id="mwClA"> "The manager of the ARPANET project, Lawrence Roberts, assembled a large team of computer scientists ... and he drew on the ideas of network experimenters in the United States and the United Kingdom. Cerf and Kahn also enlisted the help of computer scientists from England, France and the United States"</span></span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-:3-2" id="cite_note-:3-2" data-mw-footnote-number="2"><span rel="mw:referencedBy" class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwClE"><a href="#cite_ref-:3_2-0" id="mwClI"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwClM">1</span></a> <a href="#cite_ref-:3_2-1" id="mwClQ"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwClU">2</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-:3-2" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt12" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwClY" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cite web","href":"./Template:Cite_web"},"params":{"url":{"wt":"https://www.sri.com/newsroom/press-releases/computer-history-museum-sri-international-and-bbn-celebrate-40th-anniversary"},"title":{"wt":"The Computer History Museum, SRI International, and BBN Celebrate the 40th Anniversary of First ARPANET Transmission, Precursor to Today&apos;s Internet"},"date":{"wt":"27 October 2009"},"publisher":{"wt":"SRI International"},"archive-url":{"wt":"https://web.archive.org/web/20190329134941/https://www.sri.com/newsroom/press-releases/computer-history-museum-sri-international-and-bbn-celebrate-40th-anniversary"},"archive-date":{"wt":"March 29, 2019"},"access-date":{"wt":"25 September 2017"},"quote":{"wt":"But the ARPANET itself had now become an island, with no links to the other networks that had sprung up. By the early 1970s, researchers in France, the UK, and the U.S. began developing ways of connecting networks to each other, a process known as internetworking."}},"i":0}}]}'/><cite class="citation web cs1" about="#mwt12" id="mwClc"><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://web.archive.org/web/20190329134941/https://www.sri.com/newsroom/press-releases/computer-history-museum-sri-international-and-bbn-celebrate-40th-anniversary" class="external text" id="mwClg">"The Computer History Museum, SRI International, and BBN Celebrate the 40th Anniversary of First ARPANET Transmission, Precursor to Today's Internet"</a>. SRI International. October 27, 2009. Archived from <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://www.sri.com/newsroom/press-releases/computer-history-museum-sri-international-and-bbn-celebrate-40th-anniversary" class="external text" id="mwClk">the original</a> on March 29, 2019<span class="reference-accessdate" id="mwClo">. Retrieved <span class="nowrap" id="mwCls">September 25,</span> 2017</span>. <q id="mwClw">But the ARPANET itself had now become an island, with no links to the other networks that had sprung up. By the early 1970s, researchers in France, the UK, and the U.S. began developing ways of connecting networks to each other, a process known as internetworking.</q></cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Abook&amp;rft.genre=unknown&amp;rft.btitle=The+Computer+History+Museum%2C+SRI+International%2C+and+BBN+Celebrate+the+40th+Anniversary+of+First+ARPANET+Transmission%2C+Precursor+to+Today%27s+Internet&amp;rft.pub=SRI+International&amp;rft.date=2009-10-27&amp;rft_id=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.sri.com%2Fnewsroom%2Fpress-releases%2Fcomputer-history-museum-sri-international-and-bbn-celebrate-40th-anniversary&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3AHistory+of+the+Internet" class="Z3988" about="#mwt12" id="mwCl0"></span></span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-:4-3" id="cite_note-:4-3" data-mw-footnote-number="3"><span rel="mw:referencedBy" class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwCl4"><a href="#cite_ref-:4_3-0" id="mwCl8"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwCmA">1</span></a> <a href="#cite_ref-:4_3-1" id="mwCmE"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwCmI">2</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-:4-3" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt15" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwCmM" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cite web","href":"./Template:Cite_web"},"params":{"url":{"wt":"http://elk.informatik.hs-augsburg.de/tmp/cdrom-oss/CerfHowInternetCame2B.html"},"title":{"wt":"How the Internet Came to Be"},"author1":{"wt":"by Vinton Cerf, as told to Bernard Aboba"},"date":{"wt":"1993"},"access-date":{"wt":"25 September 2017"},"quote":{"wt":"We began doing concurrent implementations at Stanford, BBN, and University College London. So effort at developing the Internet protocols was international from the beginning."},"archive-date":{"wt":"September 26, 2017"},"archive-url":{"wt":"https://web.archive.org/web/20170926042220/http://elk.informatik.hs-augsburg.de/tmp/cdrom-oss/CerfHowInternetCame2B.html"}},"i":0}}]}'/><cite id="CITEREFby_Vinton_Cerf,_as_told_to_Bernard_Aboba1993" class="citation web cs1" about="#mwt15">by Vinton Cerf, as told to Bernard Aboba (1993). <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://web.archive.org/web/20170926042220/http://elk.informatik.hs-augsburg.de/tmp/cdrom-oss/CerfHowInternetCame2B.html" class="external text" id="mwCmQ">"How the Internet Came to Be"</a>. Archived from <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="http://elk.informatik.hs-augsburg.de/tmp/cdrom-oss/CerfHowInternetCame2B.html" class="external text" id="mwCmU">the original</a> on September 26, 2017<span class="reference-accessdate" id="mwCmY">. Retrieved <span class="nowrap" id="mwCmc">September 25,</span> 2017</span>. <q id="mwCmg">We began doing concurrent implementations at Stanford, BBN, and University College London. So effort at developing the Internet protocols was international from the beginning.</q></cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Abook&amp;rft.genre=unknown&amp;rft.btitle=How+the+Internet+Came+to+Be&amp;rft.date=1993&amp;rft.au=by+Vinton+Cerf%2C+as+told+to+Bernard+Aboba&amp;rft_id=http%3A%2F%2Felk.informatik.hs-augsburg.de%2Ftmp%2Fcdrom-oss%2FCerfHowInternetCame2B.html&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3AHistory+of+the+Internet" class="Z3988" about="#mwt15" id="mwCmk"></span></span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-4" id="cite_note-4" data-mw-footnote-number="4"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwCmo"><a href="#cite_ref-4" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwCms"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwCmw">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-4" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt18" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwCm0" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Cite web","href":"./Template:Cite_web"},"params":{"url":{"wt":"https://www.internethalloffame.org/blog/2015/10/19/untold-internet"},"title":{"wt":"The Untold Internet"},"date":{"wt":"October 19, 2015"},"website":{"wt":"Internet Hall of Fame"},"access-date":{"wt":"April 3, 2020"},"quote":{"wt":"many of the milestones that led to the development of the modern Internet are already familiar to many of us: the genesis of the ARPANET, the implementation of the standard network protocol TCP/IP, the growth of LANs (Large Area Networks), the invention of DNS (the Domain Name System), and the adoption of American legislation that funded U.S. Internet expansion—which helped fuel global network access—to name just a few."}},"i":0}}]}'/><cite class="citation web cs1" about="#mwt18" id="mwCm4"><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://www.internethalloffame.org/blog/2015/10/19/untold-internet" class="external text" id="mwCm8">"The Untold Internet"</a>. <i id="mwCnA">Internet Hall of Fame</i>. October 19, 2015<span class="reference-accessdate" id="mwCnE">. Retrieved <span class="nowrap" id="mwCnI">April 3,</span> 2020</span>. <q id="mwCnM">many of the milestones that led to the development of the modern Internet are already familiar to many of us: the genesis of the ARPANET, the implementation of the standard network protocol TCP/IP, the growth of LANs (Large Area Networks), the invention of DNS (the Domain Name System), and the adoption of American legislation that funded U.S. Internet expansion—which helped fuel global network access—to name just a few.</q></cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Ajournal&amp;rft.genre=unknown&amp;rft.jtitle=Internet+Hall+of+Fame&amp;rft.atitle=The+Untold+Internet&amp;rft.date=2015-10-19&amp;rft_id=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.internethalloffame.org%2Fblog%2F2015%2F10%2F19%2Funtold-internet&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3AHistory+of+the+Internet" class="Z3988" about="#mwt18" id="mwCnQ"></span></span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-5" id="cite_note-5" data-mw-footnote-number="5"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwCnU"><a href="#cite_ref-5" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwCnY"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwCnc">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-5" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt21" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwCng" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Cite journal ","href":"./Template:Cite_journal"},"params":{"date":{"wt":"2014"},"title":{"wt":"Study into UK IPv4 and IPv6 allocations"},"url":{"wt":"https://www.ofcom.org.uk/__data/assets/pdf_file/0031/37795/rtfm.pdf"},"journal":{"wt":"Reid Technical Facilities Management LLP"},"quote":{"wt":"As the network continued to grow, the model of central co-ordination by a contractor funded by the US government became unsustainable. Organisations were using IP-based networking even if they were not directly connected to the ARPAnet. They needed to get globally unique IP addresses. The nature of the ARPAnet was also changing as it was no longer limited to organisations working on ARPA-funded contracts. The US National Science Foundation set up a national IP-based backbone network, NSFnet, so that its grant-holders could be interconnected to supercomputer centres, universities and various national/regional academic/research networks, including ARPAnet. That resulting network of networks was the beginning of today&apos;s Internet."}},"i":0}}]}'/><cite class="citation journal cs1" about="#mwt21" id="mwCnk"><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://www.ofcom.org.uk/__data/assets/pdf_file/0031/37795/rtfm.pdf" class="external text" id="mwCno">"Study into UK IPv4 and IPv6 allocations"</a> <span class="cs1-format" id="mwCns">(PDF)</span>. <i id="mwCnw">Reid Technical Facilities Management LLP</i>. 2014. <q id="mwCn0">As the network continued to grow, the model of central co-ordination by a contractor funded by the US government became unsustainable. Organisations were using IP-based networking even if they were not directly connected to the ARPAnet. They needed to get globally unique IP addresses. The nature of the ARPAnet was also changing as it was no longer limited to organisations working on ARPA-funded contracts. The US National Science Foundation set up a national IP-based backbone network, NSFnet, so that its grant-holders could be interconnected to supercomputer centres, universities and various national/regional academic/research networks, including ARPAnet. That resulting network of networks was the beginning of today's Internet.</q></cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Ajournal&amp;rft.genre=article&amp;rft.jtitle=Reid+Technical+Facilities+Management+LLP&amp;rft.atitle=Study+into+UK+IPv4+and+IPv6+allocations&amp;rft.date=2014&amp;rft_id=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.ofcom.org.uk%2F__data%2Fassets%2Fpdf_file%2F0031%2F37795%2Frtfm.pdf&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3AHistory+of+the+Internet" class="Z3988" about="#mwt21" id="mwCn4"></span></span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-:2-6" id="cite_note-:2-6" data-mw-footnote-number="6"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwCn8"><a href="#cite_ref-:2_6-0" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwCoA"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwCoE">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-:2-6" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt24" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwCoI" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cite web ","href":"./Template:Cite_web"},"params":{"title":{"wt":"Origins of the Internet"},"website":{"wt":"www.nethistory.info"},"date":{"wt":"2 May 2005"},"url":{"wt":"http://www.nethistory.info/History%20of%20the%20Internet/origins.html"},"archive-url":{"wt":"https://web.archive.org/web/20110903001108/http://www.nethistory.info/History%20of%20the%20Internet/origins.html"},"archive-date":{"wt":"3 September 2011"},"url-status":{"wt":"live"}},"i":0}}]}'/><cite class="citation web cs1" about="#mwt24" id="mwCoM"><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="http://www.nethistory.info/History%20of%20the%20Internet/origins.html" class="external text" id="mwCoQ">"Origins of the Internet"</a>. <i id="mwCoU">www.nethistory.info</i>. May 2, 2005. <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://web.archive.org/web/20110903001108/http://www.nethistory.info/History%20of%20the%20Internet/origins.html" class="external text" id="mwCoY">Archived</a> from the original on September 3, 2011.</cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Ajournal&amp;rft.genre=unknown&amp;rft.jtitle=www.nethistory.info&amp;rft.atitle=Origins+of+the+Internet&amp;rft.date=2005-05-02&amp;rft_id=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.nethistory.info%2FHistory%2520of%2520the%2520Internet%2Forigins.html&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3AHistory+of+the+Internet" class="Z3988" about="#mwt24" id="mwCoc"></span></span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-7" id="cite_note-7" data-mw-footnote-number="7"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwCog"><a href="#cite_ref-7" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwCok"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwCoo">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-7" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt27" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwCos" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cite web","href":"./Template:Cite_web"},"params":{"url":{"wt":"http://www.rogerclarke.com/II/OzI04.html#CIAP"},"title":{"wt":"Origins and Nature of the Internet in Australia"},"last":{"wt":"Clarke"},"first":{"wt":"Roger"},"access-date":{"wt":"21 January 2014"},"archive-date":{"wt":"9 February 2021"},"archive-url":{"wt":"https://web.archive.org/web/20210209201253/http://www.rogerclarke.com/II/OzI04.html#CIAP"},"url-status":{"wt":"live"}},"i":0}}]}'/><cite id="CITEREFClarke" class="citation web cs1" about="#mwt27">Clarke, Roger. <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="http://www.rogerclarke.com/II/OzI04.html#CIAP" class="external text" id="mwCow">"Origins and Nature of the Internet in Australia"</a>. <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://web.archive.org/web/20210209201253/http://www.rogerclarke.com/II/OzI04.html#CIAP" class="external text" id="mwCo0">Archived</a> from the original on February 9, 2021<span class="reference-accessdate" id="mwCo4">. Retrieved <span class="nowrap" id="mwCo8">January 21,</span> 2014</span>.</cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Abook&amp;rft.genre=unknown&amp;rft.btitle=Origins+and+Nature+of+the+Internet+in+Australia&amp;rft.aulast=Clarke&amp;rft.aufirst=Roger&amp;rft_id=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.rogerclarke.com%2FII%2FOzI04.html%23CIAP&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3AHistory+of+the+Internet" class="Z3988" about="#mwt27" id="mwCpA"></span></span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-8" id="cite_note-8" data-mw-footnote-number="8"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwCpE"><a href="#cite_ref-8" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwCpI"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwCpM">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-8" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt30" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwCpQ" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cite web ","href":"./Template:Cite_web"},"params":{"url":{"wt":"http://www.indra.com/homepages/spike/isp.html"},"title":{"wt":"The First ISP"},"publisher":{"wt":"Indra.com"},"date":{"wt":"1992-08-13"},"access-date":{"wt":"2015-10-17"},"archive-url":{"wt":"https://web.archive.org/web/20160305130609/https://www.indra.com/homepages/spike/isp.html"},"archive-date":{"wt":"March 5, 2016"}},"i":0}}]}'/><cite class="citation web cs1" about="#mwt30" id="mwCpU"><a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://web.archive.org/web/20160305130609/https://www.indra.com/homepages/spike/isp.html" class="external text" id="mwCpY">"The First ISP"</a>. Indra.com. August 13, 1992. Archived from <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="http://www.indra.com/homepages/spike/isp.html" class="external text" id="mwCpc">the original</a> on March 5, 2016<span class="reference-accessdate" id="mwCpg">. Retrieved <span class="nowrap" id="mwCpk">October 17,</span> 2015</span>.</cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Abook&amp;rft.genre=unknown&amp;rft.btitle=The+First+ISP&amp;rft.pub=Indra.com&amp;rft.date=1992-08-13&amp;rft_id=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.indra.com%2Fhomepages%2Fspike%2Fisp.html&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3AHistory+of+the+Internet" class="Z3988" about="#mwt30" id="mwCpo"></span></span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-9" id="cite_note-9" data-mw-footnote-number="9"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwCps"><a href="#cite_ref-9" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwCpw"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwCp0">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-9" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt33" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwCp4" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cite book ","href":"./Template:Cite_book"},"params":{"last1":{"wt":"Couldry"},"first1":{"wt":"Nick"},"title":{"wt":"Media, Society, World: Social Theory and Digital Media Practice"},"date":{"wt":"2012"},"publisher":{"wt":"Polity Press"},"location":{"wt":"London"},"page":{"wt":"2"},"url":{"wt":"https://books.google.com/books?id=AcHvP9trbkAC&amp;pg=PA2"},"isbn":{"wt":"978-0-7456-3920-8"}},"i":0}}]}'/><cite id="CITEREFCouldry2012" class="citation book cs1" about="#mwt33">Couldry, Nick (2012). <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://books.google.com/books?id=AcHvP9trbkAC&amp;pg=PA2" class="external text" id="mwCp8"><i id="mwCqA">Media, Society, World: Social Theory and Digital Media Practice</i></a>. London: Polity Press. p.<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCqE"> </span>2. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISBN_(identifier)" title="ISBN (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCqI">ISBN</a><span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCqM"> </span><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:BookSources/978-0-7456-3920-8" title="Special:BookSources/978-0-7456-3920-8" id="mwCqQ"><bdi id="mwCqU">978-0-7456-3920-8</bdi></a>.</cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Abook&amp;rft.genre=book&amp;rft.btitle=Media%2C+Society%2C+World%3A+Social+Theory+and+Digital+Media+Practice&amp;rft.place=London&amp;rft.pages=2&amp;rft.pub=Polity+Press&amp;rft.date=2012&amp;rft.isbn=978-0-7456-3920-8&amp;rft.aulast=Couldry&amp;rft.aufirst=Nick&amp;rft_id=https%3A%2F%2Fbooks.google.com%2Fbooks%3Fid%3DAcHvP9trbkAC%26pg%3DPA2&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3AHistory+of+the+Internet" class="Z3988" about="#mwt33" id="mwCqY"></span></span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-10" id="cite_note-10" data-mw-footnote-number="10"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwCqc"><a href="#cite_ref-10" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwCqg"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwCqk">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-10" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt36" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwCqo" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Cite news","href":"./Template:Cite_news"},"params":{"last":{"wt":"Nelson"},"first":{"wt":"Patrick"},"date":{"wt":"March 20, 2019"},"title":{"wt":"Data center fiber to jump to 800 gigabits in 2019."},"work":{"wt":"Network World"},"url":{"wt":"https://www.networkworld.com/article/3374545/data-center-fiber-to-jump-to-800-gigabits-in-2019.html"}},"i":0}}]}'/><cite id="CITEREFNelson2019" class="citation news cs1" about="#mwt36">Nelson, Patrick (March 20, 2019). <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://www.networkworld.com/article/3374545/data-center-fiber-to-jump-to-800-gigabits-in-2019.html" class="external text" id="mwCqs">"Data center fiber to jump to 800 gigabits in 2019"</a>. <i id="mwCqw">Network World</i>.</cite><span title="ctx_ver=Z39.88-2004&amp;rft_val_fmt=info%3Aofi%2Ffmt%3Akev%3Amtx%3Ajournal&amp;rft.genre=article&amp;rft.jtitle=Network+World&amp;rft.atitle=Data+center+fiber+to+jump+to+800+gigabits+in+2019.&amp;rft.date=2019-03-20&amp;rft.aulast=Nelson&amp;rft.aufirst=Patrick&amp;rft_id=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.networkworld.com%2Farticle%2F3374545%2Fdata-center-fiber-to-jump-to-800-gigabits-in-2019.html&amp;rfr_id=info%3Asid%2Fen.wikipedia.org%3AHistory+of+the+Internet" class="Z3988" about="#mwt36" id="mwCq0"></span></span></li>
<li about="#cite_note-HilbertLopez2011-11" id="cite_note-HilbertLopez2011-11" data-mw-footnote-number="11"><span class="mw-cite-backlink" id="mwCq4"><a href="#cite_ref-HilbertLopez2011_11-0" rel="mw:referencedBy" id="mwCq8"><span class="mw-linkback-text" id="mwCrA">↑</span></a></span> <span id="mw-reference-text-cite_note-HilbertLopez2011-11" class="mw-reference-text reference-text"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1333433106" about="#mwt39" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles mw:Transclusion" id="mwCrE" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Citation/CS1/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""},"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"cite journal ","href":"./Template:Cite_journal"},"params":{"last1":{"wt":"Hilbert"},"first1":{"wt":"Martin"},"last2":{"wt":"López"},"first2":{"wt":"Priscila"},"title":{"wt":"The World&apos;s Technological Capacity to Store, Communicate, and Compute Information"},"journal":{"wt":"Science"},"date":{"wt":"April 2011"},"volume":{"wt":"332"},"issue":{"wt":"6025"},"pages":{"wt":"60–65"},"doi":{"wt":"10.1126/science.1200970"},"pmid":{"wt":"21310967"},"bibcode":{"wt":"2011Sci...332...60H"},"s2cid":{"wt":"206531385"},"doi-access":{"wt":"free"}},"i":0}}]}'/><cite id="CITEREFHilbertLópez2011" class="citation journal cs1" about="#mwt39">Hilbert, Martin; López, Priscila (April 2011). <a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://doi.org/10.1126%2Fscience.1200970" class="external text" id="mwCrI">"The World's Technological Capacity to Store, Communicate, and Compute Information"</a>. <i id="mwCrM">Science</i>. <b id="mwCrQ">332</b> (6025): <span class="nowrap" id="mwCrU">60–</span>65. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bibcode_(identifier)" title="Bibcode (identifier)" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCrY">Bibcode</a>:<a rel="mw:ExtLink nofollow" href="https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2011Sci...332...60H" class="external text" id="mwCrc">2011Sci...332...60H</a>. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="h